    {
      "id": 50,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        40,
        -640
      ],
      "size": [
        720,
        720
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 50,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "## How to make videos\n\n1. In the **bigLust** recipe (other tab), click your picture(s), then\n   **Send to Video Queue**.\n2. Here: click a slot, type the MOTION in the right prompt box (what should\n   move), pick a size / length, press **Run**.\n3. Each clip **auto-saves** to `output/freedom_video/`. STEP 9 also has\n   **Set User Save Folder** / **Save to my folder**, and **Extend video\n   length** (joins more onto the end).\n\n## Start the launcher in VIDEO MODE first\nThe big model needs almost all your graphics memory. In the launcher pick\n**\"Start VIDEO mode\"** (or press **v** in the launcher console). That turns\noff the talking + listening apps and runs this app in low-memory mode, so\nthe model fits. In the normal full stack this workflow is ~40x slower - not\nworth running.\n\n## This is the big model, sped up\nIt's the 14-billion model (real-looking motion), but with a **speed LoRA**\nbolted on (STEP 2a / 2b) so it runs in **4 steps** instead of 20.\nDefault clip = **832x480, 3.4 seconds** (the model's natural clip length).\nIn Video Mode that takes about **2.5 minutes**. Bigger or longer costs\nproportionally more. The trade for the speed LoRA: motion is a touch calmer.\nGood enough for almost everything.\n\n## The other two files\n- `Freedom_Video_14B_quality.json` - same model, speed LoRA OFF, 20 steps.\n  Slowest, best. Use it for a hero shot (Video Mode only).\n- `Freedom_Video_5B_fast.json` - the small model. ~1-3 min, softer motion,\n  almost no LoRA support - but it works even in the full stack.\n"
      ],
      "title": "READ ME FIRST  (14B + Lightning, fast)",
      "color": "#1b3a1b",
      "bgcolor": "#20401f"
    },
    {
      "id": 51,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        780,
        -40
      ],
      "size": [
        430,
        140
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 51,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "14B is split in two. **1a** handles overall movement and layout, **1b**\nsharpens the fine detail. Both load; STEP 7 uses one then the other. You\nnever change these two boxes."
      ],
      "title": "STEP 1 - the two experts",
      "color": "#233",
      "bgcolor": "#355"
    },
    {
      "id": 55,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        780,
        -500
      ],
      "size": [
        430,
        420
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 55,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "These are two engines for turning a picture into a moving clip.\n\n**The big engine (this workflow)**\n- The movement looks real. Faces stay faces, hands stay hands, nothing\n  melts or wobbles between frames.\n- It listens. Type a detailed motion and it actually does that motion.\n- The add-on \"trick\" files - eyes rolling back, ecstasy face, finger\n  movement - are almost all built for this big engine. The small one\n  barely has any.\n- It works in two passes: first pass blocks out the big motion, second\n  pass cleans up the detail.\n\n**The catch, and the fix**\nRaw, the big engine is slow: 8-15 min for a 5-second clip. So this file\nadds a **speed LoRA** (STEP 2a / 2b) that cuts it to **4 steps / 2-4\nmin**. Motion gets a little calmer; everything else stays.\n\n**Which file to use**\n- Quick idea / does-it-move check -> `Freedom_Video_5B_fast`.\n- Normal use -> stay here (14B + speed LoRA).\n- One perfect hero shot, time no object -> `Freedom_Video_14B_quality`."
      ],
      "title": "WHY THE BIG MODEL (14B), NOT THE SMALL ONE (5B)",
      "color": "#2a2f3a",
      "bgcolor": "#3c4a63"
    },
    {
      "id": 52,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1240,
        -60
      ],
      "size": [
        460,
        120
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 52,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "**STEP 4 (green)** fills from the queue slot - type in the queue panel,\nnot here. Write what MOVES. **STEP 5 (red)** is ignored while CFG is 1\n(the speed LoRA needs CFG 1) - just leave it."
      ],
      "title": "STEP 4-5 - the words",
      "color": "#233",
      "bgcolor": "#355"
    },
    {
      "id": 53,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1240,
        720
      ],
      "size": [
        460,
        360
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 53,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "Each expert has **two** LoRA boxes stacked on it. A LoRA on 14B must go\non **both** experts, so everything here comes in pairs.\n\n### 2a / 2b  -  SPEED LoRA (Lightning)  -  leave ON\nThis is what makes the clip take 4 minutes instead of 15. Green = on.\nDon't touch it unless you're switching to the quality file.\n\n### 2c / 2d  -  MOTION / EXPRESSION LoRA  -  OFF, for you to fill\nThis is where a \"trick\" LoRA goes (eyes roll back, ecstasy face, finger\nmovement, a specific thrust motion). Get a **Wan 2.2 / Wan 14B** one (see\n`comfyui_ext/LORA_SOURCES.md`), drop it in the loras folder, then on\n**both** 2c and 2d: right-click -> **Mode -> Always**, pick the same file,\nand put its trigger word in your motion prompt.\n\n**strength_model = volume knob.** `0.0` off. `~0.5` gentle (use if the\nface distorts). `1.0` normal, start here. `1.2-1.5` crank it (only if too\nweak). Left = subtle, right = strong, too far right = broken. Keep 2c and\n2d the same number."
      ],
      "title": "STEP 2 - the LoRA stack  (read this)",
      "color": "#3a2a1a",
